import
	../../html

intro-new html[]()
	- section "new and list-new",
		- p quote
			The function for creating a new value is usually called {fn-new} or {fn-list-new},
			and there is a special syntax for calling these functions.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				# Same as 'new 1, 2, 3'
				tuple (nat, nat, nat) = 1, 2, 3
				log tuple
				# Same as 'list-new (1, 2, 3)'
				array nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				log array

		- aside-small quote
			Tuples will be explained in {intro-link tuples} and arrays in {intro-link collections}.
			{br}
			The focus here is on the syntax.

		- p "The syntax {num-1-2-3} may call either:"
		- bullets
			- "A {fn-new} function taking those arguments, making it equivalent to {fn-new}{sp}{num-1-2-3}"
			- "A {fn-list-new} function taking with an array containg {num-1-2-3}"
		- p quote
			{fn-new} is for functions that expect a fixed number of arguments.
			{br}
			{fn-list-new} is for functions that take a variable number of arguments.
		- p quote
			{num-1-2-3} is a call expression.
			Keen will build the initial overload set using all functions named {fn-new} or {fn-list-new},
			then go through the same logic described in {intro-link overloading}.

		- p quote
			{fn-foo}{sp}{num-1-2} and {fn-foo}{sp}{parenthesized num-1-2} are two different expressions.
			{br}
			The former just calls {fn-foo}. The latter calls {fn-new} first, then {fn-foo} on the result.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo (1, 2)
				log foo 1, 2

			foo nat(tuple (nat, nat))
				tuple.a + tuple.b
			foo nat(a nat, b nat)
				a * b

	- section "More \"new\" syntax",
		- subsection "Single-argument new",
			- p "To call {fn-new} with a single argument, end with a trailing comma."
			- keen-runnable quote
				main void()
					xs nat[] = 1,
					log xs

		- subsection "Empty new",
			- p "To call {fn-new} with no arguments, write {parens}."
			- keen-runnable quote
				main void()
					xs nat[] = ()
					log xs

		- subsection "Implicit empty new",
			- p "In many contexts fn-new with no arguments will be called implicitly."
			- keen-runnable quote
				main void()
					xs nat[] = if false
						1, 2
					log xs

	- section "Defining a \"new\" function",
		- p "{fn-new} functions are just ordinary functions and are declared in the usual way."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (1, "hello", true)::nat

			new nat(a nat, b string, c bool)
				a + b.size + c.to

		- aside-small quote
			Normally you wouldn't define {fn-new} for {nat},
			but we haven't introduced user-defined types yet.

	- section "Defining a \"list-new\" function",
		- p "{fn-list-new} functions are also just ordinary functions."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (1, 2, 3)::string

			list-new string(values nat[])
				to-json values

		- p "Keen generates an array from all the arguments before calling {fn-list-new}."

	- section "Multi-line new",
		- p quote
			There is an equivalent syntax for calling {fn-new} or {fn-list-new}:

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				xs nat[] =
					- 1
					- 2
					- 3
				log xs

		- p quote
			This behaves the same as {num-1-2-3}, just with a multi-line syntax to make it convenient for bigger calls.
		- p quote
			The extra indentation is not needed when it is the return value of a function:
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log xs

			xs nat[]()
				- 1
				- 2
				- 3

	- section "Multi-line new with arrays",
		- p quote
			Using {keyword "--"} instead of {keyword "-"}
			concatenates all the elements of an array into the array passed to {fn-list-new}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				xs nat[] = 2, 3
				log
					:: nat[]
					- 1
					-- xs
					- 4

		- aside-small quote
			Since the number of arguments can't be known ahead of time,
			a multi-line new with any {keyword "--"} lines can only call {fn-list-new},
			never {fn-new}.

		- p "This can be used to conditionally include elements in an array."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log
					:: nat[]
					-- if true
						- 1
					-- if false
						- 2
					- 3

	- section "Named-new",
		- p quote
			The syntax {var-x}{colon}{num-1}{comma}{var-y}{colon}{num-2} calls either:
		- bullets
			- "A {fn-new} function with parameters named {var-x} and {var-y}."
			- "A {fn-named-new} function taking an array of names and an array of values."
		- p quote
			Keen will build the initial overload set with both kinds of function,
			then filter using the logic described in {intro-link overloading}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				# This calls 'named-new'
				a json = x: 1, y: 2
				log a

		- p "This has a multi-line form which is usually preferred."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log a

			a json()
				x: 1
				y: 2

		- p "To demonstrate with a custom {fn-new}:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (x: 1, y: 2)::nat

			new nat(x nat, y nat)
				x + y

		- p "To demonstrate with a custom {fn-named-new}:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (x: 1, y: 2)::string

			named-new string(names symbol[], values nat[])
				names.to-json ~~ values.to-json
